{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Then Sarah denied, etc.</b> The reason for His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, and His also saying ״כִּי יָרֵאָה״ — is to make known the righteousness of Sarah our mother, the righteous woman, that she did not say explicitly ״לֹא צָחַקְתִּי״ so as not to bring forth from her mouth a false matter, but rather she said something from which it is understood that her intention was to say she had not laughed. Therefore it does not say ״וַתֹּאמֶר לֹא צָחַקְתִּי״. And from another angle, one may ask how she could have had the audacity to deny the words of HaShem — therefore it says <b>כִּי יָרֵאָה</b> — meaning: you should know that a faithful servant who fears her master, yet inadvertently did something improper, and her master rebukes her for what she did — when the magnitude of her fear grows in her heart over having transgressed her master's will, she cannot muster the strength to admit what she did, because of the magnitude of the fear, and she will deny; and in her very denial she indicates that the matter was indeed true, but because of the magnitude of the fear she cannot summon the strength to face it openly that she indeed did so. And this is what the verse informs us: ״כִּי יָרֵאָה״. Understand this. And Abraham said to her <b>לֹא כִּי צָחָקְתְּ</b> — meaning: it is far more fitting that you admit that you laughed, for in this HaShem desires that a person confess orally and explicitly. And so did our Prophet, upon him be peace, instruct them that this is the desired thing: ״וּמוֹדֶה וְעֹזֵב יְרֻחָם״ (Proverbs 28:13)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:04.279336454Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}